WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.260
On a desert morning, a basking iguana is warmer than the air.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.660
So is a mouse nearby.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.840
Yet the iguana makes far less heat of its own than the mouse.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.840
How can both be warmer than the air?

5
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.130
Picture each body as a tank of warmth.

6
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.610
The level in the tank is body temperature.

7
00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.730
Heat flows in, heat leaks out, and the level settles where the two match.

8
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.260
Two taps fill the tank.

9
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.720
The orange tap is heat made by the body's own metabolism.

10
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.340
The yellow tap is heat gained from outside, from sunlight or a warm rock.

11
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.280
The blue outlet is the dry leak.

12
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.140
Its width is conductance, how readily heat escapes for each degree of difference.

13
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.320
The flow also grows with the gap between body and air.

14
00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.090
Three routes make up that leak.

15
00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:02.170
Radiation, conduction, and convection by moving air or water.

16
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.250
Each can run in or out, so the leak is their net.

17
00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.330
Sunlight and a warm rock count once, in the yellow tap, not the leak.

18
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:14.443
A fourth outlet is evaporation.

19
00:01:15.903 --> 00:01:20.183
Water turning to vapour carries heat away, as in sweating or panting.

20
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:24.583
In practice it only removes heat, and it costs water.

21
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.367
So the level sits where heat in equals heat out.

22
00:01:29.727 --> 00:01:34.507
Body temperature rises above the air by the heat coming in, minus evaporation,

23
00:01:34.507 --> 00:01:36.327
divided by conductance.

24
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.570
The iguana's orange tap runs small.

25
00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.110
Its yellow tap pours, from the sun above and the rock below.

26
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.890
That inflow alone lifts it well above the air.

27
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:51.073
The mouse has almost no yellow tap.

28
00:01:52.353 --> 00:01:56.173
Its orange tap runs strong, and fur keeps its blue outlet narrow.

29
00:01:57.553 --> 00:02:00.093
Its own metabolism holds it above the air.

30
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:05.357
That is the real difference between an ectotherm and an endotherm.

31
00:02:06.237 --> 00:02:10.177
The words name where the heat comes from, not how warm the animal is.

32
00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:14.427
Now move the mouse into colder air, with the same fur.

33
00:02:15.627 --> 00:02:17.927
The gap widens, and heat leaks faster.

34
00:02:19.247 --> 00:02:21.067
Name two ways it can hold its level.

35
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:30.153
Make more heat, by opening the orange tap.

36
00:02:31.373 --> 00:02:35.333
Or narrow the leak, by closing skin blood vessels and fluffing the fur.

37
00:02:36.653 --> 00:02:39.613
Every dial an animal has changes one of these terms.

38
00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:44.247
Who turns the taps?

39
00:02:45.707 --> 00:02:49.887
Sensors in the skin and deep in the body report to a controller in the brain.

40
00:02:51.247 --> 00:02:54.987
In mammals it sits in the preoptic area of the hypothalamus.

41
00:02:54.987 --> 00:02:57.847
It chooses the target temperature.

42
00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.050
Skin sensors give early warning.

43
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:07.330
Cold air on the skin can start heat-saving responses before the core has cooled at

44
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:07.590
all.
